Article 5: Where any of the following circumstances exists, and the lessor requests dissolution of the financial leasing contract, the People's Court shall support it:
(1) The lessee fails to pay rent in accordance with the time limit and amount stipulated in the contract, which satisfies the dissolution conditions stipulated in the contract, and still fails to pay within a reasonable period after the lessor's demand;
(2) The contract does not expressly stipulate the circumstances for dissolution due to arrears of rent, but the lessee's arrears of rent reach two or more installments, or the amount reaches 15 percent or more of the total rent, and the lessee still fails to pay within a reasonable period after the lessor's demand;
(3) Other circumstances in which the lessee violates the provisions of the contract, causing the purpose of the contract to be unattainable.

Article 8: Where the leased property does not conform to the provisions of the financial leasing contract and the lessor has committed one of the following acts, and the lessee, in accordance with the provisions of Articles 744 and 747 of the Civil Code, requires the lessor to bear corresponding liability, the People's Court shall support it:
(1) The lessor plays a decisive role in the selection of the leased property when the lessee chooses the seller or the leased property;
(2) The lessor interferes with or requires the lessee to choose the seller or the leased property in accordance with the lessor's wishes;
(3) The lessor unilaterally changes the seller or the leased property already selected by the lessee.
Where the lessee claims that it determined the leased property in reliance on the lessor's skill or that the lessor interfered with the selection of the leased property, the lessee shall bear the burden of adducing evidence for the above facts.
